# Negative-control mutant — the payment-method form validates before it writes (AC-8, R35)
#
# Target (correct line): `settingPaymentMethod.validating.onError` in the
#   `changingPaymentMethod` region — an invalid model must land in the form's
#   `available.error` node with NO `PATCH` leaving the module. This mutant routes
#   that `onError` straight to `updating`, so a schema-invalid model is written
#   anyway (validation skipped).
#
# STILL UNREACHABLE (re-checked this pass, AFTER the loadLookups isReady fix).
#   Fix 1 makes `contract.services.ts` `loadLookups` await
#   `usePaymentDetails().isReady()` and read its `data`, so the contract manager
#   now DOES stand up the stored-cards query. But the contract integration
#   harness (contract.int-helpers.ts) mocks only the `PATCH .../payment_details`
#   — no stored-methods GET list is served — so that reused query resolves empty
#   and the payment-method schema still carries NO `paymentDetailsId` enum. With
#   no enum, no id is schema-invalid, and `set({})`/`set({paymentDetailsId:null})`
#   is coerced back to the loaded default by the parser. So no invalid model can
#   reach `validating`, `validatePaymentMethod` never rejects, `onError` never
#   fires, and this mutation flips NOTHING in the contract suite. Confirmed: the
#   whole suite carries no assertion that submits a schema-invalid payment method
#   and expects a 422 in the form error region with no PATCH (grep over
#   contract/__tests__). The populated-enum invalid-id rejection is proven in the
#   payment-details module's own suite.
#
# To make this mutant pinnable, the prover must land a contract-harness test that
#   (a) serves a stored payment_details LIST fixture so the schema enum populates,
#   then (b) submits an id not in that enum and asserts `hasError`/422 with no
#   `payment_details` PATCH. That test is the one this mutant then flips red.
#   Until then the invalid-enum proof stays the payment-details suite's, and this
#   remains a SURFACED gap, not a hidden one.
#
# Apply with:  git apply <this file>   (run from repo root)
# Revert with: git apply -R <this file>
diff --git a/packages/headless/src/modules/contract/contract.machine.ts b/packages/headless/src/modules/contract/contract.machine.ts
